# HashMap vs Frequency Array

| Feature | HashMap | Array |
|----------|---------|--------|
| Character Set | Any Unicode | Fixed (e.g., 26 lowercase) |
| Average Lookup | O(1) | O(1) |
| Memory | Higher | Lower |
| Speed | Slightly Slower | Faster |
| Interview Preference | Generic solution | Preferred for fixed alphabets |

---

# Complexity Analysis

## HashMap

| Time | Space |
|------|-------|
| O(N) | O(K) |

Where `K` is the number of distinct characters.

---

## Frequency Array

| Time | Space |
|------|-------|
| O(N) | O(1) |

Because the array size (26) is constant.

---

# Common Mistakes

## Forgetting Length Check

Wrong

```text
abc

ab
```

Without checking lengths, unnecessary work is performed.

Always compare lengths first.

---

## Using One Frequency Array Incorrectly

Wrong

```java
count[s.charAt(i)]++;
```

This uses the character's Unicode value directly and will cause an `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` with a 26-element array.

Correct

```java
count[s.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
```

---

## Ignoring Character Set

The array solution only works when the input is limited to lowercase English letters.

If the input can contain:

- Uppercase letters
- Unicode
- Emojis
- Multiple languages

use a `HashMap<Character, Integer>`.

---

# Edge Cases

### Empty Strings

```text
